1
00:00:11,370 --> 00:00:12,990
I think that once you learn

2
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:14,910
about the second law of thermodynamics,

3
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:16,800
it can be a little depressing.

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:17,633
You've been told

5
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:19,980
that there's a fundamental feature of reality:

6
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:22,290
that disorder increases,

7
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,690
that things just wind down, right?

8
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:26,520
That batteries run out,

9
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:28,290
that cream and coffee mix together,

10
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:29,880
that ice cubes melt.

11
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:33,450
It's kind of a depressing view of the future.

12
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:35,970
How did all of this interestingness

13
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:37,830
come about in the first place?

14
00:00:37,830 --> 00:00:41,010
If the whole thing that reality does

15
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:44,910
is just move closer and closer to complete disorder,

16
00:00:44,910 --> 00:00:47,910
then how did something as exquisitely organized

17
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:49,983
as a human being come about?

18
00:00:52,710 --> 00:00:55,320
This idea that there's this tension

19
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:58,500
between the organization of things in the Universe

20
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,380
and the natural evolution of things

21
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:03,090
became a little bit sharper

22
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:04,740
over the course of the 1800s

23
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:06,660
because that's when we put together

24
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:09,690
this idea that entropy increases all the time.

25
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:12,930
Things tend to go from orderly to disorderly

26
00:01:12,930 --> 00:01:16,500
just because there are many more ways to be disorderly.

27
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,830
This is a deep down law of nature.

28
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:23,040
It implies what we call the 'Heat death of the Universe':

29
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:24,690
that all the stuff you see,

30
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:27,990
the engines, the burning stars, the living beings,

31
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:32,190
these all represent systems that are increasing

32
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:34,080
the overall entropy of the Universe.

33
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:36,990
And if you think, which is probably true,

34
00:01:36,990 --> 00:01:39,780
that there's a maximum entropy you can reach,

35
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:43,140
a maximum level of disorderliness and chaos,

36
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:44,580
eventually we will get there

37
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:48,360
and all the interestingness in the Universe will be gone.

38
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:51,180
The Universe will reach what we call an 'equilibrium':

39
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:53,160
a state of maximum chaos

40
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:56,310
and nothing interesting happening anymore.

41
00:01:56,310 --> 00:01:59,070
One of the issues with really feeling

42
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:01,650
through the implications of the second law of thermodynamics

43
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:06,360
is that people tend to mix up simple versus complex

44
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:08,220
and orderly versus disorderly.

45
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:10,590
The truth is these are two different axes,

46
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:13,200
two different ways of thinking about something.

47
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,490
The increase of entropy

48
00:02:14,490 --> 00:02:16,860
says that we go from orderly to disorderly,

49
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:20,370
but it says nothing about simple versus complex.

50
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:22,530
This is a new scientific question

51
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:24,600
that we are facing right now:

52
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:29,600
'What is the journey from low entropy to high entropy like

53
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:32,850
and how is it affected by the laws of physics

54
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:36,210
so that the actual path it takes

55
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:38,823
leads to complex structures?'

56
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:43,080
One of the difficulties in figuring out,

57
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:46,770
specifically, how life here on Earth came into being

58
00:02:46,770 --> 00:02:49,620
is that it's not just a random sort of complex system.

59
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:51,360
It's a very specific thing.

60
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:54,810
And life as we know it now involves different aspects,

61
00:02:54,810 --> 00:02:56,370
all of which are important.

62
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:58,500
You need replication, right?

63
00:02:58,500 --> 00:02:59,790
You need Darwinian evolution.

64
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:00,930
We have DNA.

65
00:03:00,930 --> 00:03:03,540
We have a genome that gets replicated,

66
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:05,520
not perfectly, but pretty well.

67
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:07,560
You need compartmentalization.

68
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,450
Every cell has a cell wall

69
00:03:09,450 --> 00:03:11,340
so you can separate the living cell

70
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:12,810
from the rest of the world.

71
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:14,820
And also you need metabolism, right?

72
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:15,900
You need that fuel,

73
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:17,940
you need that low-entropy energy

74
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:19,800
that we can use to keep ourselves going

75
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:23,190
and then expel to the world in a higher-entropy form.

76
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:25,350
So, which came first?

77
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:28,950
This is why explaining the beginning of complex structures

78
00:03:28,950 --> 00:03:29,783
is always hard,

79
00:03:29,783 --> 00:03:32,550
because they all seem to depend on each other.

80
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:35,370
There is a replication-first camp

81
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:38,513
in the origin of life studies literature that says,

82
00:03:38,513 --> 00:03:42,090
"Look, clearly the genetic information is necessary

83
00:03:42,090 --> 00:03:43,470
to call it life,

84
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:44,580
that must have come first

85
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:46,710
and it must have hooked up to an energy source."

86
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:49,593
But there's also a metabolism-first camp that says,

87
00:03:49,593 --> 00:03:50,901
"Look, it doesn't matter

88
00:03:50,901 --> 00:03:52,380
if you have information sitting there,

89
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:54,930
if it's not going someplace, if it's not doing anything,

90
00:03:54,930 --> 00:03:57,480
if it's not moving around and metabolizing,

91
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:59,280
you can't call it life."

92
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:00,420
And the nice thing to me

93
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:02,610
about the metabolism-first point of view

94
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:06,120
is that you can kind of see how it might arise

95
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:10,530
out of purely physical, non-biological processes

96
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:13,410
because remember, to maintain its orderliness

97
00:04:13,410 --> 00:04:14,760
and its complexity,

98
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:18,360
living beings need to increase the entropy of the Universe,

99
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:21,963
they need to feed off of low-entropy energy.

100
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:24,000
So this was an idea

101
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,300
that a number of biologists and geologists had.

102
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:29,580
And on the basis of it, they made predictions.

103
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:30,510
They said, you know,

104
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:33,270
"Life is not gonna form in some warm pond."

105
00:04:33,270 --> 00:04:34,530
This was Darwin's idea,

106
00:04:34,530 --> 00:04:36,150
that maybe you just put all the stuff together,

107
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:36,983
all the ingredients,

108
00:04:36,983 --> 00:04:40,380
and eventually a little bug crawls out, okay?

109
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:41,820
Or a little bacterium.

110
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:43,530
And they said, "Look, that's never gonna happen,

111
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:46,110
because there's no increasing entropy.

112
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:47,010
What you need is

113
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:50,850
just the right biochemical, geological arrangement of things

114
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:54,450
to have these long, sophisticated, complex reactions happen

115
00:04:54,450 --> 00:04:58,590
that can then be captured into the first living organism."

116
00:04:58,590 --> 00:04:59,670
And they thought about

117
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:02,670
what kind of conditions would they be,

118
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:06,540
and they thought that it might be a warm, hydrothermal vent

119
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:08,100
under the oceans, right?

120
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:09,930
With certain chemical balances.

121
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:10,763
And I'm a physicist,

122
00:05:10,763 --> 00:05:12,810
I don't know the details and I shouldn't say anything,

123
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:15,960
but the point is these kinds of hydrothermal vents

124
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:17,820
were not known at the time,

125
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:19,530
they had not yet been discovered,

126
00:05:19,530 --> 00:05:21,150
but a prediction was made-

127
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:22,800
these must exist

128
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:25,380
in order for life to have come into existence.

129
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:28,530
And after the prediction was made, we found them.

130
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:30,720
Famously, the 'Lost City' formation

131
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,430
at the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean

132
00:05:32,430 --> 00:05:34,140
was found by submersibles

133
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:36,420
going down near the bottom of the ocean floor.

134
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:39,180
They found exactly the kind of geochemical conditions

135
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:40,800
that had been hypothesized

136
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:43,590
as possible places where life could have formed.

137
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,240
Does that mean it's right?

138
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:47,040
No, but it's a little bit of evidence

139
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:49,170
that it could be on the right track.

140
00:05:49,170 --> 00:05:51,450
The origin of life, I think, personally,

141
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:55,110
is one of the most important unanswered scientific questions

142
00:05:55,110 --> 00:05:56,550
but it's one that we're absolutely

143
00:05:56,550 --> 00:05:58,083
making progress on right now.

